<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesoamerican codices</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesoamerican codices are folding books painted on bark paper or deerskin by the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, recording history, religion, calendars and tribute. Many were destroyed by Spanish missionaries after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. The surviving codices, including some made under Spanish rule, are key sources for Aztec history and culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesoamerican-codices.jpg" alt="Mesoamerican codices" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tlakwilo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Examples_of_Mesoamerican_pictorial_manuscripts_(facsimiles).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name covers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the indigenous pictorial tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, whether in content, style or symbolic conventions. Some use Mesoamerican writing systems, but others are pure pictures, native maps without glyphs, or colonial alphabetic texts with indigenous illustrations. Besides the best-known Aztec, Maya and Mixtec codices, important manuscripts were made by the Tlaxcaltec, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> and Cuicatecs. Only about twenty pre-Columbian codices are known to survive.</p><p>Strictly, pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are not codices, a word popularised in the 19th century. They took native forms: the tira, a long strip of hide or paper such as the Codex Boturini; the screenfold, a tira folded into a book, such as the Codex Borgia; the roll, such as the Selden Roll; and the lienzo, a sheet of cloth, such as the Lienzo de Quauhquechollan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Donald Robertson and John B. Glass, who made the first comprehensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, sorted the documents into traditional pictorials, the paintings and maps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relaciones-geograficas">Relaciones Geográficas</a> made for the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1579–1585, the Techialoyan manuscripts drawn up as legal documents for indigenous communities, pictorial catechisms, and forgeries. By origin they range from pre-Columbian works such as the Borgia group to codices made under Spanish patronage, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza">Codex Mendoza</a>, and native colonial ones such as the Codex Xolotl. Their subjects include ritual and the calendar, history, genealogy, maps and tribute. Traditional pictorials numbered 434 in the original census, and more are still found in Mexican villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Scholars agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maya-script">Maya writing</a> is logo-syllabic, but there is no consensus on how picture and writing relate in the non-Maya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, which are the great majority. Some specialists see a mixture of iconography and true writing, others a system of graphic communication that represents language only in part. Some documents, such as the Codex Borgia, cannot yet be assigned to any language. Because the manuscripts are fragile, conservators now favour non-invasive methods, and digitisation has made them available to a wider public.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesoamerican codices” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
